comments.text:
The consensus seems to be based mainly on two seat models from PollCheck (+3.9) and Britain Predicts (\~+5). I can't see any other direct sources that bookmakers would be pricing from?

Seat models are less accurate than constituency polls, and even those this parliament have missed by 8-12 points in both directions:

* Gorton: Greens polled 28-33, ended up with 40.7&#x20;
* Makerfield: Labour polled under 50, got 54.8

A 4 point lead with a 10 point error bar means that polling-based odds should start roughly around 65%.

I pull myself *slightly* towards Green because Labor's lost 5/9 in Kellner's left-challenger group, and because of Feinstein's endorsement.&#x20;

Then I pull myself a bit more back towards Labour due to Greens 12-month national low rating, Burnham still being net positive, and no third-party Reform threat to increase tactical voting. The Labour candidate also seems stronger than weaker.&#x20;

\---

I think a constituency poll should move the numbers a lot from here. If it comes out Labor +3 or something, this could be a tossup. Labor nationally is indeed trending positive so that kind of result would be a bit surprising, though.&#x20;
